  {
    "index": 3,
    "title": "187. Different Layers",
    "transcript": "(gentle music) -: Now till this point, we have talked about one of the Stereotype Annotation, which is your Component. So if you want Spring to manage your class or the object, we have to write @Component on top of it, right? Now, this is not the only Stereotype Annotation available. See, if we talk about any application, you'll be having multiple layers there. Now, this is not a web application, this is a simple console-based application. But let's say if you have a full-fledged application, you'll be having multiple layers. So normally, you'll be having a Client. Now, Client will interact with the Server. And of course, when you build an application, you will also have a Database there, right? And of course, when we talk about web application, we'll be talking about Spring Web later. But just to give you a scenario, we have a Client, we have a Server in between, and then we have a Database. Now in this Server as well, we have multiple layers. Now, when you want to work with data, of course, that's why we have Database there. And in every application you'll be having some kind of Database. Now, if you want to fetch this data, so let's have a, if a client wants a data, the request goes to the Server, Server sends a request to the Database, and Database says, \"Okay, that's your data.\" Get your data back. Data goes to the Server, Server sends that data to the Client. Now, of course, depend upon Client, it can be a webpage, it can be only JSON data, but ultimately, it is Server who is responsible to accept and return data. Now, even this Server will have multiple layers. So you'll be having a Controller. Again, we'll talk about this thing in detail later, when we talk about Spring MVC, but we have a Controller. Now, Controller job is to only work with the request, so whatever request you send from the Client. And if you want someone to handle that, you will do it with help of Controller. Now, Controller says, \"My only job here is to accept the request and send data back. My job is not to do any processing.\" What if you want to do any processing? In that case, we have to use something called a Service. Now, Service is a layer who is responsible to do any kind of processing. Let's say, if you want to add two numbers, if you want to find a Factorial, if you want to find a Logic. Let's say, \"Which is a best stock to invest in?\" If you want to sort the product based on their average rating. So all the thing will be done in the Service class. Now, what if you want some data from Database? So let's say you're saying, \"Hey, you know what? I want to find best products within the range of $10 to $100. Now, at this point, you don't have your data in your application, you have that in your Database. But where you will do this processing? You will do the processing inside Service. But unfortunately, Service don't have a data. Now, Service has to connect with Database. Now, in that case, you'll be having a layer in between, which will be your DAO layer, which is called Data Access Object, or we can say Repository. Now, this is a layer who is responsible to interact with Database, get the data, give it to the Service. Now, Service will do some processing on that, and then give the data back to the Controller. So how is the flow? From the Client, data goes to the Controller, Controller says Service, Service sends to Repository, from Repository, request goes to Database. Database says, \"Okay, this is your data.\" Repository sends back data to the Service, Service does the processing, give it back to the Controller, and Controller give it back to the Client. That's the entire flow here. So that means we have multiple layers, right? Of course, we'll talk about Controller later, but we have two more layers here, which we'll talk about, the Service and Repository. The job of Service layer is to do only processing part, not interact with the Client or not interact with Database. Repository's job is to connect with Database and get the data. Now, if you want to create those classes in Java, which annotation you're going to use? In fact, let me just do that here. So what I will do is I will create, I will add extra layers here. So let me close this desktop. Let's only work with laptop here. I don't need all these things open. Let me close everything. So let's say we only have laptop here. And imagine we do have a database for laptop somewhere, okay? So how will we create this layer? So this is your, imagine this is your Client layer, okay? Or maybe you can imagine this as a Controller if you want, but at this point, Client make much, much more sense. So let's say this is your Client layer. From this layer, you are requesting to the Server. Now on Server, let's replace Controller. The request directly goes to a Service layer. Now, what Service will do? Let's say you want 10, you want data about laptop. Now the Client sends request to the Service. Service will say, \"Okay, I will give you the data, but I have to get data from the Repository.\" So you'll be having one more layer there. But how do we create those layers, and how do we annotate that? Let's do the code in the next video."
  },
