    "index": 5,
    "title": "189. Repository Layer",
    "transcript": "(bright music) -: Okay, let's work with the repository layer. Now this should be short, okay, there's some problem with the laptop service, I entered one there, okay, you know that. So now if you want to work with database, what we can do is we can create another class here, and we'll name this class as maybe laptop repository. And this class is responsible to connect with database. Of course, you can write some methods here. Let's say public void safe. And you can take a laptop object, let's say lab. And then in this you can write your database's steps. Of course, we are not going to write this in this video, but later on we'll do that. So we'll basically write our database's steps here. Don't write any database's steps in the service class. This is a class responsible to connect with database, right. And you can have all the current operations here, create, read, update, delete, and whatever extra you want to have. So this is a class where you can do everything. And from the service, basically, you don't need to, I mean, you will not print method call, we need to call the method save of repository, right. But then how do you call the save method? And we have to also pass the lab, let's pass lab, yeah. So we have to call this method save. How do you call this method save here? Now, to call the method, of course you need object of the laptop repository. So let's do that here. So I will say private laptop repository. I will say repo. And then I want to inject it. Of course, we can use setter injection, but let's say if I'm using the field injection, so I'm injecting this object by using autowired. And now I can just use this repo.save. And what I will do here is I will just print saved in database. That's it, nothing fancy. I'm just trying to say print something. Imagine I'm writing all the database steps and it is executing the query, saving the data in the database, and you got response as saved. Imagine everything, okay. And now let's run this and let's see what happens. If you run this code, you will get an error. I mean, that's what I'm expecting. Okay, so you can see we got an error. It says the laptop repository could not be found, the bean not found. It's because on top of this, we have to write an annotation. As I mentioned before, you can also write at component, it will work. So if I run this with add component, so you can say it works, but then when you have a repository layer, instead of using component or service, we are going to use a repository as an annotation. It does the same thing. It is also implementing component. It has an annotation component on top of it. But again, if you are using any domain-driven design, it makes sense to use repository. And also for developers who will be seeing your code later, if they see repository, then it makes sense to them that, yeah, this is a repository layer. And if you're on this code, it will work the same way. But now if you can differentiate, we have laptop service or we have service annotation, we got laptop repository, we have a repository. In fact, let's create a separate project based on all these things which we have learned because slowly we are moving towards actually connecting with database, right. So let's use different example next time, maybe for student instead of alien and laptop, and see how do you manage students using different layers, right. But yeah, 'til this point we have talked about service layer, repository layer. And one thing we forgot, we should be saving this in a different package called repo. Move to repo, yeah. So you can see we have different folders. Now, this will make much more sense once we understood Spring Web, because that's where we are going to literally create all these packages and save our files there. I know you might be thinking in one folder you've got only one file, as you can see. But in real project, you'll be having multiple entities, multiple objects to work with. So they will have multiple classes here, multiple interfaces. So yeah, that's about this particular topic of service and repository. See you in the next video."
